18|The Group Project Problem|小组作业怎么分工|校园生活|海报作业快到期限，两个朋友重新商量分工。|分配任务、说明进度、提出帮助
project=项目作业|outline=提纲|deadline=截止时间
What have we finished so far?|Could you handle the pictures?|Let's make a simple plan.
lucy: Little Moments. The Group Project Problem. Emma and I are making a poster about our neighborhood. Yesterday, we both thought the other person was bringing the pictures. Today, we have a beautiful blank sheet of paper.
emma: What have we finished so far?
lucy: The title. And I brought a very nice marker.
emma: Great. At this speed, we'll finish by the time we're eighty.
lucy: Let's make a simple plan. I open my notebook and draw two columns. We need pictures, short descriptions, a map, and a little time to put everything together.
emma: I can draw the map. I actually like drawing maps. Could you handle the pictures?
lucy: Sure. I've already taken photos of the park and the library. I can print them tonight. We each choose two places to write about, then agree to bring everything tomorrow.
staff: How's the project going over here?
emma: Much better now. We finally gave the jobs to actual people instead of hoping they would do themselves.
staff: That's usually helpful. Leave time to check the whole poster before you hand it in.
lucy: The next day, Emma brings the map, and I bring the pictures. We lay everything out before we start gluing. One picture is upside down, and one street has an extra letter. We fix both.
emma: Look at that. A real poster.
lucy: And we're nowhere near eighty. I put a check beside every job on our list. Even the nice marker finally gets something useful to do.
===
19|A Table by the Window|想坐窗边的位置|吃饭与购物|餐厅没有空窗边桌，Lucy学会在等待和先入座之间选择。|询问等位、表达偏好、确认人数
table=餐桌|available=可用的|wait=等待
A table for three, please.|How long is the wait?|This table is fine.
lucy: Little Moments. A Table by the Window. Mom, Ben, and I stop at a diner for lunch. I spot a table by the window with sunlight falling across it. Unfortunately, another family spots it about three seconds before I do.
mom: A table for three, please.
clerk: Of course. We have a booth available near the back.
lucy: Could we sit by the window instead?
clerk: You can, but those tables are all taken right now.
mom: How long is the wait?
clerk: Probably fifteen or twenty minutes. I can't promise an exact time.
ben: My stomach says that is a very long time.
lucy: I look at the booth near the back. It has soft seats and a tiny picture of a dog wearing sunglasses above it. Actually, this table is fine.
clerk: Right this way. Here are your menus. I'll give you a few minutes.
lucy: We slide into the booth. Ben immediately points at the dog picture and decides it's the owner of the restaurant.
mom: A dog who runs a diner? That's a busy dog.
ben: He probably checks all the hamburgers personally.
lucy: We order lunch and share a glass bottle of ketchup. By the time our food arrives, I have completely forgotten about the window. The booth is comfortable, Ben is telling dog stories, and Mom looks relaxed.
mom: Still wish we had waited?
lucy: Not at all. This table comes with entertainment. I point to Ben. Unfortunately, there doesn't seem to be an off switch.
===
20|Please Don't Feed the Ducks|给鸭子当观众|周末与朋友|姐弟俩在公园发现标牌，换一种方式亲近鸭子。|读标牌、提醒规则、提出替代活动
sign=标牌|pond=池塘|watch=观察
What does that sign say?|Let's watch them from here.|That one looks like it's in a hurry.
lucy: Little Moments. Please Don't Feed the Ducks. Ben and I walk around the park pond with Dad. Ben is carrying the last piece of his sandwich. When three ducks swim toward us, he starts pulling the bread into little pieces.
dad: Hold on a second. What does that sign say?
lucy: Please don't feed the ducks. Ben looks at the bread, then at the ducks. They seem extremely interested in ignoring the sign.
ben: But they came all this way. It feels rude not to offer them anything.
dad: The park asks visitors to let the ducks find their own food. We can enjoy watching them without feeding them.
lucy: Let's watch them from here. We sit on a bench a little way back from the water. Ben keeps his sandwich for himself, and I start counting ducks.
ben: That one looks like it's in a hurry.
lucy: A small duck paddles past a much bigger one and disappears behind some reeds. Maybe it's late for duck school.
dad: Or maybe it just knows a good place to look for lunch.
lucy: We see one duck shake water off its feathers and another tuck its head down for a rest. Suddenly, watching them feels more interesting than throwing bread.
ben: The sleepy one is me on Monday morning.
lucy: On the way out, we put our sandwich wrapper in a trash can. Ben waves at the pond. The ducks don't wave back, but one gives a very loud quack. We decide that counts.
===
21|The Missing Water Bottle|水杯失物招领|校园生活|Lucy在失物招领处用颜色和细节找回水杯。|描述失物、提供特征、确认归属
lost and found=失物招领处|sticker=贴纸|lid=盖子
I think I left it in the gym.|What does it look like?|That's the one!
lucy: Little Moments. The Missing Water Bottle. After gym class, I reach into the side pocket of my backpack. No water bottle. I check the other side, even though it has never lived there. Still nothing.
emma: When did you last have it?
lucy: Before basketball. I think I left it in the gym. We go back, but the bench is empty. Emma suggests checking the school office, where there's a box for lost things.
staff: Looking for something?
lucy: My water bottle. I had it this morning, and now it's missing.
staff: What does it look like?
lucy: It's dark blue with a silver lid. There's a little moon sticker on one side and a scratch near the bottom.
staff: Let's see. We have two blue bottles here. Does either of these look familiar?
lucy: The first one has a red lid. The second has my little moon, slightly peeling at one corner. That's the one!
emma: Your moon brought you home.
staff: Is your name on it anywhere?
lucy: Not yet. I should probably fix that.
staff: A name label would make it easier to return next time.
lucy: At home, I dry the bottle and add a small label under the moon. Ben offers to write an exciting name instead of Lucy. Later, I tell Emma about it on the phone.
emma: Like what?
lucy: Captain Splash. I decide Lucy is more useful for the school office. Captain Splash can be its name at home, where nobody needs to fill out a lost-and-found form.
===
22|The Video Call With Grandma|给奶奶打视频电话|家庭日常|一家人视频聊天，先解决镜头角度和听不清的小问题。|检查声音、调整镜头、分享近况
camera=摄像头|volume=音量|hear=听见
Can you hear us now?|Move the camera a little higher.|What have you been up to?
lucy: Little Moments. The Video Call With Grandma. Every Sunday, Mom calls Grandma, and we all squeeze onto the couch to say hello. Today, Grandma can see the ceiling, the top of Dad's head, and absolutely none of us.
mom: Move the camera a little higher. No, the other way. There. Now I can see everybody.
lucy: Mom is talking to Dad, who is balancing the tablet against a pillow. Grandma waves on the screen, but we can't hear her yet.
dad: I turned the volume down earlier. Let me fix that.
lucy: Can you hear us now? Grandma gives us a thumbs-up. She says she can hear Ben better than anyone, which is not surprising. He's sitting closest to the microphone.
ben: Hi, Grandma! I lost a tooth!
mom: You don't have to shout. She's far away, but the microphone is right here.
lucy: Grandma laughs and asks us what we've been doing. Mom points the camera toward me. What have you been up to? That's Grandma's favorite question, and this time I have something ready to show her.
ben: Lucy made a poster. I helped by not spilling anything on it.
lucy: A very valuable contribution. I hold up the poster and tell Grandma about our neighborhood project. Then Ben shows the gap in his teeth from three different angles.
dad: Same time next Sunday?
lucy: Grandma nods. We wave goodbye, and Dad carefully moves the tablet off the pillow. Next week, we'll try to show her our faces right from the beginning.
===
23|The Backpack That Wouldn't Close|拉不上拉链的书包|校园生活|Lucy带了太多东西，和爸爸一起整理书包。|确认需要、取出多余物品、整理空间
zipper=拉链|pocket=口袋|leave behind=留下不带
Do you need all of that?|Let's take everything out.|That can stay at home.
lucy: Little Moments. The Backpack That Wouldn't Close. It's Sunday evening, and my backpack refuses to close. I pull the zipper, push on the top, and try asking nicely. Nothing works. Dad walks past my door and stops.
dad: Do you need all of that?
lucy: Probably. Maybe. I'm prepared for everything.
dad: Let's take everything out. Then we can see what everything actually means.
lucy: We put my things on the bed. Three notebooks, two library books, a pencil case, a sweater, an old art project, and a rock that looks a little like a potato.
dad: Is the rock part of your homework?
lucy: No. I found it last week. It has a nice personality.
dad: Then it can keep your desk company. That can stay at home.
lucy: I check tomorrow's schedule. I only need two notebooks, and the art project was finished days ago. One library book is due tomorrow, so that goes back in.
ben: Can I have the potato rock?
lucy: You may visit it on my desk. It's not ready to move out. I put the heavier books close to the back and the smaller things in the front pocket. The zipper finally closes without a fight.
dad: How does it feel now?
lucy: Much better. I lift the backpack with one hand, then try it on. Ben watches carefully from the doorway.
ben: Your bag looks happier.
lucy: So do my shoulders. The rock remains on my desk, looking quietly supportive. Tomorrow, I will be prepared for school, which is probably enough.
===
24|A Haircut, Just a Little|头发只剪一点点|出行与办事|理发前说清楚想剪多少，比只说一点点更有用。|描述长度、澄清程度、确认效果
haircut=理发|trim=修剪|shoulder=肩膀
Just a little off, please.|About this much?|I'd like to keep it long.
lucy: Little Moments. A Haircut, Just a Little. Mom takes me to the hair salon after school. I sit in a big chair while the stylist puts a cape around my shoulders. In the mirror, I look like a person wearing a very fancy tablecloth.
clerk: What would you like today?
lucy: Just a little off, please.
clerk: Sure. About this much?
lucy: She holds her fingers a short distance apart. Maybe a little less. I'd like to keep it long.
clerk: No problem. I'll trim the ends and keep it below your shoulders. Does that sound right?
lucy: Yes, exactly. I feel better now that we've agreed on what a little means. Mom sits nearby, reading a magazine with a very serious cake on the cover.
mom: Comfortable?
lucy: Mostly. My nose has decided to itch, and my hands are trapped under this cape.
clerk: You can move your hand out. Just tell me before you move your head.
lucy: I scratch my nose, and the haircut continues. Small pieces of hair fall to the floor. When the stylist finishes, she gives me a hand mirror so I can see the back.
clerk: What do you think?
lucy: I like it. It still feels like my hair, just a little neater.
mom: Ready to go?
lucy: Almost. I turn my head once more in the mirror. A little was exactly the right amount. Next time, I'll use my fingers to show it instead of expecting someone to guess.
===
25|The Rainy Walk Home|雨天一起回家|出行与办事|放学下雨，两个朋友商量怎样共用一把伞。|提出同行、提醒注意、商量路线
umbrella=雨伞|puddle=水坑|shortcut=近路
Do you want to share my umbrella?|Let's take the longer way.|You're getting wet on that side.
lucy: Little Moments. The Rainy Walk Home. School ends, and rain is falling hard outside the front doors. Emma opens her umbrella. I open my backpack and find a notebook, a snack, and no umbrella at all.
emma: Do you want to share my umbrella?
lucy: Yes, please. Mine is doing an excellent job of staying dry at home. We step outside together and immediately discover that walking under one umbrella takes teamwork.
emma: You're getting wet on that side. Come a little closer.
lucy: I move closer. Our backpacks bump. We take three steps and bump again. After a minute, we find a rhythm that doesn't involve walking into each other.
emma: Should we take the shortcut through the park?
lucy: Let's take the longer way. The path gets muddy, and I don't want to bring half the park home on my shoes.
emma: Good point. Sidewalk it is.
lucy: At the corner, we stop before a giant puddle. I go around the left side while Emma starts going right. The umbrella can't decide who to follow.
emma: Same side, Lucy. We need to choose the same side.
lucy: Right. This is more complicated than our group project. We laugh and carefully walk around together. When we reach my house, Mom opens the door with a towel.
mom: Come in, both of you. You look like you've had an adventure.
lucy: A very small, very wet adventure. Tomorrow, my umbrella is coming to school. It has enjoyed enough time off.
